Financial markets shift through behavioral regimes driven by changing risk appetite, and these shifts are often visible in narratives before they fully show up in price indicators. However, most operational risk frameworks still depend on lagging signals 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
like realized volatility and the VIX, which makes it easier to explain regime changes after they happen than to spot them early for risk management. Major events like the COVID-19 crash, the 2021 GameStop surge, and the 2022 crypto winter highlight this p
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 at\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 tern: narrative intensity and cross-market sentiment stress increase quickly as market structures become unstable.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid2847553 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid10881906 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa278\sl276\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10881906 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid10881906\charrsid10881906 \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
Despite growth in financial NLP, most sentiment systems are limited to single assets, narrow source types, or dashboard-style workflows that do not directly produce reproducible regime labels. Traditional regime models also face stability issues; Hidden M
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
arkov frameworks are often sensitive to local noise and tend to over-switch, reducing their usefulness. Meanwhile, aligning mixed-frequency data remains a major technical challenge because text events are irregular while market bars are regular, and cross
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 -a\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 sset spillover effects are poorly captured by isolated models.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
This study proposes a practical two-layer cross-asset architecture to address these issues. The first layer develops aligned volatility and sentiment features from market and text data, while the second layer applies a Statistical Jump Model to maintain p
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 ersistence and prevent frequent switches. This design aims to move beyond single-market sentiment scoring toward a regime-based system that can be audited, rerun, and interpreted consistently ac
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 ross Equities, Crypto, Forex, and Commodities.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
The goal is to test whether cross-asset sentiment structures can offer lead or transition insights that complement traditional volatility measures. In this framework, H1 assesses global lead-time behavior against VIX-based stress; H2 examines divergence p
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 atterns near regime shifts; and H3 investigates the connectedness across stable and transitioning regimes.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 This research provides a reproducible end-to-end framework with established artifacts, walk-forward eva\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
luation, and protocol-driven hypothesis testing. The results support divergence and connectedness mechanisms (H2 and H3), while the expected global lead-time advantage over VIX (H1) was not confirmed under the fixed criteria. Therefore, the study limits i
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 ts conclusions to findings directly supported by the evidence base.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid10881906 
