The primary goal of regime detection is to enhance risk management\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
shielding portfolios during unfavorable conditions while remaining exposed during favorable ones. Incorporating sentiment signals into risk management systems requires understanding how se\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
ntiment fluctuations relate to extreme market events.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 Shu et al. (2024) showed that their statistical jump model specifically aimed to reduce downside risk. The JM-guided strategy achieved volatility declines of about 2-3 percentage points compared to buy-a
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
nd-hold, with maximum drawdown improvements of 10-15 percentage points across the tested equity indices. The approach experienced smaller drawdowns during major stress periods and offered stronger protection against negative market moves.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
Cao et al. (2025) analyzed sentiment connectedness and stock price crash risk through network analysis of S&P 500 stocks. They built sentiment spillover networks using nonlinear Granger causality and assessed firm-level sentiment interconnectedness throug
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 h various network \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
centrality measures (degree, closeness, betweenness, eigenvector centrality). Firms with higher sentiment connectedness showed increased crash risk because they both spread and absorb irrational sentiment signals more intensely. Importantly, sentiment con
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
nectedness proved to be a better predictor of crash risk than individual firm sentiment, especially during extreme market conditions. Stock return synchronicity strengthened the sentiment-crash link, while conservative accounting practices lessened it.

\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 Nyak\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
urukwa and Seetharam (2025) expanded sentiment network analysis by applying TVP-VAR frequency-connectedness across DJIA components. Their analysis broke down sentiment connectedness into short-term (1-5 days), medium-term (5-20 days), and long-term (20+ d
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
ays) segments. Major findings included that sentiment shocks mainly transfer in the short term, negative news sentiment shows higher connectedness than positive sentiment (aligning with negativity bias in media), and sentiment connectedness peaks during g
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 lo\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 bally significant events like COVID-19. These time-based dynamics suggest that tracking short-term shifts in sentiment connectedness could provide early warnings of regime stress.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
In our framework, these insights support incorporating sentiment network metrics as indicators of regime shifts. Sudden increases in cross-asset sentiment connectedness could signal impending regime instability, while divergence patterns (some assets disc
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 onnecting from the sentiment network) might indicate rotation opportunities o\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 r upcoming shifts contagion}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid13633873\charrsid1736181 .}{\rtlch\fcs1 
