\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa240\sl276\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1668116 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid1668116\charrsid1668116 \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
Despite significant progress in financial NLP and econometric modeling, a crucial methodological gap persists at the intersection of generative sentiment analysis and regime detection. While Konstantinidis et al. (2024) successfully established FinLlama a
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 s a superior sentiment classifier for algorithmic trading\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 demonstrating a 44.7% return improvement over FinBERT\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 
their approach was limited to standard portfolio construction rules and did not address identifying structural market regimes. Conversely, Shu \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 et\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
 al. (2024) validated Statistical Jump Models (JMs) as a reliable alternative to Hidden Markov Models, showing that jump penalties significantly reduce "whipsaw" signals and downside risk. However, their implementation relied solely on endogenous price fe
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 atures (returns and volatility), overlooking the predictive power of exogenous sentiment signals.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 Additionally, while recent studies such as Yang et al. (2025) have applied LLMs to cross-asset monitoring, and Shi (2025) has integrated GARCH-MIDAS with senti\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 
ment analysis, no existing research has directly incorporated FinLlama's semantic depth into the persistence-enforcing framework of Statistical Jump Models for a cross-asset universe. This research addresses this gap by developing the first Two-Layer Sent
\hich\af0\dbch\af31501\loch\f0 iment Regime Detector, which identifies leading indicators of structural breaks by combining generative AI sentiment with econometric jump penalties across Equities, Crypto, Forex, and Commodities}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
